// The compiler's version
// ======================
// Which release this compiler is, said in one place and read from wherever the
// answer matters. Today that is two places: `hive version`, which is this
// question asked directly, and `hive container`, which pins the compiler its
// Dockerfile downloads to this one rather than to whatever is newest.
//
// **The tag is what decides it.** Pushing `v0.1.10` writes `v0.1.10` in here
// before anything is compiled, so the release and the compiler it publishes
// agree by construction rather than by somebody remembering — see the workflow
// in `.github/workflows/build.yml`.
//
// What is committed below is therefore a floor rather than a contract: it is
// what a compiler built from a checkout says, and a checkout is not a release.
// It names the release behind it, which is the nearest true thing there is to
// say — a working tree has no binaries to download, and the release before it is
// the last compiler that did. Stale here costs a Dockerfile written by an
// unreleased build one version of drift; it can never make a *release* wrong,
// because a release overwrites it.
//
// A line of source rather than something read from the environment while the
// compiler runs, for two reasons. `./selfhost` proves the compiler is a fixpoint
// by compiling it twice and diffing the Go, and a version that arrived from
// outside would make two builds of one tree differ. And the answer has to
// survive into a binary somebody else downloads: whatever `hive container` knows
// about the compiler, it has to know on a machine where nothing was exported.

/// The release this compiler is, spelled the way the tag is — `v0.1.9`, which is
/// also how a release's URL spells it.
func current(): Str {
	return "v0.1.9"
}
